{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—AI systems capable of autonomously generating, executing, and debugging code—has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful capabilities introduce significant security and safety concerns that demand rigorous evaluation. This paper surveys recent research advances in code agents and the emerging field of red-teaming for evaluating their security properties. We discuss the dual nature of code agents, highlighting both their transformative potential and the risks they pose when misused or compromised. We review red-teaming methodologies specifically designed to assess code agent vulnerabilities, including benchmark frameworks for evaluating risky code execution and generation. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation approaches and proposes directions for future research to ensure the safe deployment of code agents in real-world applications.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how we interact with software development tools. By combining large language models (LLMs) with code interpreters and execution environments, these agents can autonomously perform complex programming tasks, debug code, generate tests, and even develop entire applications from high-level specifications. This capability has found applications across industries, from automating routine coding tasks to accelerating software prototyping.\n\nHowever, the same capabilities that make code agents powerful also introduce novel security challenges. Code agents execute arbitrary code, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the agent is manipulated into generating or running malicious payloads. This risk is exacerbated by the increasing integration of code agents into production environments and development pipelines.\n\nRed-teaming—the practice of adversarially testing systems to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent safety. Unlike traditional red-teaming approaches that focus on language models' text generation capabilities, red-teaming for code agents must evaluate both the code generation and code execution dimensions of these systems.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive review of research advances in code agents and their associated red-teaming methodologies. We organize our discussion around the key challenges in code agent security, existing evaluation benchmarks, and promising directions for future research.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Agent Architectures\n\nRecent work has explored various architectural designs for code agents. Modern code agents typically integrate LLMs with tools such as code interpreters, file system access, and external APIs. These agents can reason about code, execute it, observe results, and iteratively refine their solutions. The integration of these capabilities enables agents to solve complex programming tasks that would be challenging for static code generation models.\n\n### Red-teaming for Code Safety\n\nThe security evaluation of code agents has gained significant attention in recent research. **Guo et al. [1]** introduced RedCode, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating the safety of code agents. As stated in their work, \"With the rapidly increasing capabilities and adoption of code agents for AI-assisted coding, safety concerns, such as generating or executing risky code, have become significant barriers to the real-world deployment of these agents.\" Their benchmark specifically targets risky code execution and generation, providing a standardized evaluation framework for assessing code agent vulnerabilities.\n\nBuilding on this foundation, subsequent work has developed more sophisticated red-teaming approaches. **Guo et al. [2]** presented RedCodeAgent, an automatic red-teaming framework that systematically probes diverse code agent architectures for vulnerabilities. This approach moves beyond manual prompt-based red-teaming to enable scalable, automated security evaluations.\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment of LLMs\n\nThe broader field of LLM safety has also contributed methodologies applicable to code agents. Research on trustworthiness assessment of LLMs has established evaluation frameworks for measuring model behavior across various dimensions including safety, honesty, and robustness. These methodologies provide valuable foundations for developing comprehensive evaluation protocols for code-specific applications.\n\n### Challenges and Open Questions\n\nDespite these advances, significant challenges remain in code agent security research. Key open questions include:\n\n1. **Evaluation Scope**: Current benchmarks may not cover the full spectrum of potential vulnerabilities in code agents, particularly those arising from complex interactions between multiple tools and APIs.\n\n2. **Adaptive Adversaries**: Most red-teaming approaches use static or semi-static attack strategies. Developing frameworks for adaptive red-teaming that learn from agent defenses could provide more thorough evaluations.\n\n3. **Defense Mechanisms**: While red-teaming identifies vulnerabilities, systematic development of defense mechanisms for code agents remains an active research area.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents has made remarkable progress in enabling autonomous code generation and execution. However, these capabilities come with significant security responsibilities. Red-teaming methodologies have emerged as essential tools for evaluating code agent safety, with benchmarks like RedCode providing standardized evaluation frameworks.\n\nAs code agents become more prevalent in software development workflows, the research community must continue to advance both the capabilities of these agents and the methodologies for ensuring their safe operation. Future research should focus on developing more comprehensive benchmarks, automated red-teaming frameworks, and effective defense mechanisms that can scale alongside the capabilities of code agents.\n\nOnly through rigorous and continuous evaluation can we ensure that code agents fulfill their promise of transforming software development while minimizing security risks. The collaboration between code agent developers, security researchers, and the broader research community will be essential to achieving this goal.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.12345*, 2024.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat /app/paper.txt"
}'}

